const proseTitle = 'The 808 is the instrument, not the bass';
const proseBody = `
  <p>Trap is built from the low end up. The 808 carries the melody as much as the keys do, which is why out-of-tune 808s are the first beginner mistake and why glides between notes matter more than the drum pattern. Most trap sits between 130 and 160 BPM counted in half time, with the snare or clap landing on beat 3 rather than 2 and 4, so a 140 BPM beat still feels slow and heavy. The hi-hats do the opposite job: straight 8ths or 16ths as the floor, with triplet rolls and the odd 32nd burst as the movement.</p>
  <p>Sonura generates those parts as <strong>separate stems</strong>: the <strong>808</strong>, the <strong>drums</strong>, the <strong>melody</strong> and the <strong>FX</strong>, each on its own track. That is the difference from a flat-file generator in practice. You can retune a single 808 note, shorten the notes for more bounce, swap the clap, or keep the bell melody and drop everything else for the intro. Export the stems through <a href="/features/stem-export/">stem export</a> and finish in FL Studio, Ableton or Logic the way you would with your own drums, or build the drum layer first with the free <a href="/tools/ai-beat-maker/">beat maker</a>.</p>
  <p>The sound palette follows the Atlanta template that Lex Luger and Metro Boomin set: a long, saturated 808 tuned to the key, a tight snare layered with a clap, crisp hats panned for width, and a dark minor melody on piano, bells, strings or pads with plenty of space in it. The models are trained on ethically licensed data, so a beat you sell on BeatStars or lease to an artist is clear from the first generation. Want straight boom bap drums instead of the half-time feel? Switch to the <a href="/genre/hip-hop/">rap beat maker</a>. Want the London version with sliding 808s and a swung hat? Try the <a href="/genre/drill/">drill beat maker</a>.</p>
`;

